+# Design
+
+## Current behavior
+
+`CategoryServiceImpl.delete` collects the category lineage, finds products in the subtree, removes some category links, and deletes products when no links remain. The category entity already models product membership as a many-to-many association, so the deletion must sever those links before deleting categories. The REST facade currently resolves a missing id through a path that can return null and then reports success.
+
+## Approach
+
+1. Resolve the category by merchant and fail with `ResourceNotFoundException` when it is absent.
+2. Build the complete subtree, including the root, and load products associated with those category ids.
+3. For each product, remove only categories in the subtree and persist the product whenever its category set changes; never call product deletion.
+4. Delete descendant categories before the root so the existing parent cascade/foreign-key relationships are safe, preserving the category descriptions and join rows through the normal entity lifecycle.
+5. Keep public category and product filtering backed by live category associations so deleted ids naturally return 404/empty results.
+6. Add focused service and API integration tests, including cart/order fixtures that use an affected product.
+
+## Error handling and compatibility
+
+Use the existing `ResourceNotFoundException` mapping for both missing deletion targets and subsequent category reads. Keep the successful delete response unchanged. Product, cart, and order schemas and APIs remain unchanged.
